45:Assemble the Codex runtime in this order:
46-
47-1. identify the dominant control problem:
48-   - code / patch / debug
49-   - design / route / recommendation
50-   - evidence / retrieval / synthesis
51-   - evaluation / release gate / comparison
52-   - orchestration / lifecycle / delegation
53:2. select one primary skill based on that dominant control problem, not on the richest available surface
54-3. choose the lightest base prompt that still preserves the needed control depth
55-4. attach overlays only when they materially change evidence authority, topology, safety, adaptation, or evaluation quality
56-5. choose at most one compact packet per unresolved control boundary such as route, lifecycle, adaptation, quality gate, approval, recovery, capability contract, evidence boundary, or memory scope
57-6. if the task is coding-heavy, keep the briefing package explicit enough to show the active slice, human brief, and quality-gate owner
